<section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-16">
  <div class="prose-content space-y-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--fg-muted)]">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl font-semibold text-[var(--fg)]">
      A free online file editor and file compressor, built around one idea
    </h2>
    <p>
      realfileeditor is a free online <strong class="text-[var(--fg)]">file editor</strong> for the
      everyday jobs people actually run into with PDFs, images and documents: a file that's too big to
      email, a scan that needs its pages reordered, a PDF that needs a page rotated the right way up. Most
      tools that call themselves a "file editor online" or a "free file editor" ask you to pick a quality
      preset — low, medium, high — and hope the result lands where you need it. realfileeditor works the
      other way around. Every compressor here starts from the file size you actually want and works
      backward to reach it, using a draggable gauge instead of a guess.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--fg)]">A PDF file editor without the software install</h3>
    <p>
      As a <strong class="text-[var(--fg)]">PDF file editor</strong>, realfileeditor covers the tasks
      people search for most: compressing a PDF down to a target size, merging several PDFs into one in a
      specific order, splitting a long PDF into a page range or equal chunks, rotating sideways pages, and
      stamping a watermark across every page. Unlike a desktop PDF file editor or an Adobe subscription,
      nothing to install and nothing to configure — open the tool, drop in a file, and the processing
      happens right in your browser. That also means it works as a
      <strong class="text-[var(--fg)]">free PDF file editor</strong> on a Chromebook, a work laptop with
      restricted software installs, or a phone, since it's a website rather than an application.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--fg)]">A file compressor that doesn't leave the final size to chance</h3>
    <p>
      The <strong class="text-[var(--fg)]">file compressor</strong> tools follow the same target-first
      approach. Compressing an image or a PDF means dragging a gauge to the exact size you need — under an
      upload portal's limit, under an email attachment cap, or under a
      <strong class="text-[var(--fg)]">Discord file compressor</strong> limit for sharing in a server —
      instead of repeatedly re-running a "high compression" preset and checking whether it was enough. For
      files that don't need re-encoding, the
      <a href="/zip-files" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">Zip Files tool</a> acts as a straightforward
      <strong class="text-[var(--fg)]">free file compressor</strong> for bundling a batch of documents,
      images or PDFs into a single .zip to send or store together.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--fg)]">Who this file editor online is for</h3>
    <p>
      realfileeditor is built for anyone who needs a quick, reliable
      <strong class="text-[var(--fg)]">file editor online</strong> without signing up for an account: a
      student compressing a scanned assignment, someone applying for a job through a portal with a strict
      file-size limit, a small business owner merging invoices into one PDF, or anyone sharing a batch of
      images who wants them zipped into one download. Every tool is free to use on a single file, with no
      watermark added to your output and no software to install.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--fg)]">Built to be honest about what it can and can't do</h3>
    <p>
      Not every format is supported yet — audio files, STL, PSD and JSON aren't handled by this toolkit
      today, and we'd rather say that plainly than pretend otherwise. Where a tool has a real limitation,
      like how small a text-heavy PDF can realistically compress, the tool tells you what it actually
      achieved rather than showing a number it can't back up. That's the same philosophy behind the whole
      site: a file editor and file compressor that sets expectations honestly and gets the everyday job
      done in your browser, for free.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--fg)]">No account, no watermark, no waiting</h3>
    <p>
      There's no sign-up wall between you and any tool on this page — open a tool, drop in a file, and
      you're working. Because compression and conversion run locally in your browser rather than on a
      remote server, there's also no upload queue to wait behind, which matters most when the file
      compressor or file editor is the last step before a deadline. If you use realfileeditor often
      enough that an account, saved history or higher batch limits would help, that's the direction we're
      building toward next — for now, the goal is a fast, free, no-friction file editor online that does
      exactly what it says on the button.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
